  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 216 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nBroad long wave trough over the east coast will see a weak \nshortwave rotate through it that will bring some extra support for\nthunderstorms along a stalled frontal boundary across parts of \nnorth and central Georgia. Surface dewpoints will be in the 60s \nand 70s allowing for decent instability to develop via surface \nheating. Storms from this will still be ongoing at the beginning \nof the long term on Sunday evening and continue into the early \novernight hours, especially in parts of central Georgia.\n\nMonday will see a backdoor front continue to slide into the CWA. \nThere is some ensemble spread on just how quickly this front \nslides into the area, which would have some impacts on what things\nlook like on Monday. The latest NBM mean is a bit cooler showing \nhighs in the mid 80s, but has a 10th percentile in the 70s across \neastern Georgia and a 90th approaching or exceeding 90 degrees. \nExperience would tell me to lean towards the current mean that \ncame in a bit lower as the front likely will be moving in a bit \nfaster than most models have progged. Any PoPs will likely be \nfocused along the front, with best chances currently across east \ncentral Georgia. Expect to see some potential changes in the PoPs \nas confidence in the timing and location of the front grows.\n\nFor the rest of the long term through the end of next week, \nmodels show the deep ridge over the middle of the country getting \npumped up by a deep trough/TPV over the west coast that tapping \ndirectly into tropical moisture associated with Hurricane Marie. \nAnticyclonic rossby wave break along the anticyclonic side of the \njet happens over the Midwest as the trough ejects northeastward on\nTuesday. This means the upper level ridge sets up over the south.\nThis is a lot of fancy meteorology talk to explain why the heat \nwill once again start building through the area into the end of \nnext week, with highs well into the 90s. One saving grace may be \nthat moisture takes a bit longer to return, keeping the heat \nindices below triple digits until the very end of the week.",
